Introduction
A double top or double bottom is easy to see after the fact but hard to trade in real time: peaks rarely match exactly, the valley or ridge between them can be shallow, and human eyes tend to impose tidy shapes on noisy price action. For algorithmic developers the problem is worse. Without fixed rules the same pattern appears and disappears depending on who drew the neckline, which bars count as pivots, or how lenient the tolerance is. That non-determinism makes signals unreproducible in the Strategy Tester, encourages post-hoc parameter fitting, and defeats robust automation.
In our
previous article (Part 52)
, we automated the tCISD model, which pairs Quarterly Theory cycles with a correlated-symbol divergence. This article fixes the problem above by translating the pattern into a strict, testable contract. We build a
MetaQuotes Language 5
(MQL5) program that formalizes every judgment: confirmed swing pivots, a quantitative peak-matching tolerance scaled by pattern height, a neckline defined from the intervening pivot, configurable spacing and leg-balance filters, and explicit trigger rules (close through the neckline or optional pullback). The program also defines stop placement (stop beyond the paired extreme with a buffer), target rules (measured-move or reward-to-risk), lot sizing modes, and optional trailing stops. The result is a reproducible detection, trigger, and risk-management pipeline you can run in the Strategy Tester and tune without falling back on visual judgment. We will cover the following topics:
Understanding the Double Top and Double Bottom Reversal Model
Implementation in MQL5
Backtesting
Conclusion
By the end of this article, you will be able to:
Detect double tops and double bottoms from confirmed swing pivots and validate each with peak tolerance, leg balance, and spacing filters.
Mark the peaks and neckline on the chart and enter a reversal on the break or an optional pullback, with the stop beyond the pattern extreme and a measured-move or reward-to-risk target managed by a trailing stop.
Understanding the Double Top and Double Bottom Reversal Model
A
double top and a double bottom
are the market's way of failing twice at the same price. In a double top, price rallies to a high, pulls back, and rallies again to roughly the same high before turning down, and the failure of that second push to make new ground signals that buyers are exhausted. The double bottom is the mirror image: two troughs at about the same low, where sellers fail to break lower the second time and control passes back to buyers. The line drawn through the reaction point between the two peaks is the neckline, the level the whole pattern hinges on, because the pattern is only complete once price closes through it.
A rule-based approach outperforms visual judgment because it turns each subjective decision into a parameter. Peak matching becomes a tolerance scaled by pattern height, and leg balance compares bar counts on each leg to reject lopsided shapes. Spacing and an optional prior-trend requirement filter out peaks too close together or patterns that appear mid-range with nothing to reverse. Once a pattern passes the filters, the anchors are explicit: the neckline is the trigger, the paired extreme anchors the stop, and the neckline-to-extreme distance defines the target. The break of the neckline confirms the reversal, and an optional pullback to the retested level offers a second, often better-priced entry. The general strategy architecture is shown below.
When trading the model, wait for the second peak to form and confirm it stalls near the first rather than pushing decisively past it. Treat the neckline as your line in the sand, and let a close through it, not a mere touch, mark the reversal. Decide in advance whether you take the break directly or hold out for the pullback, which fills less often but at a tighter risk. Anchor your stop just beyond the twin peaks so an invalidation is unambiguous, and project the pattern height from the neckline for a first target that reflects the size of the structure you traded. Skip patterns with no clear trend leading into them, because a reversal needs something to reverse. In a nutshell, here is a visualization of what we will create.
Implementation in MQL5
Before beginning the implementation, it is worth fixing what the program will count as a pattern, because every function below exists to serve these rules. A swing pivot is confirmed only once a set number of bars have closed on both sides of it with none printing a higher high or lower low. Two peaks pair into a double top or double bottom only when their prices sit within a tolerance expressed as a percentage of the pattern height, so one setting means the same thing on a tall pattern and a shallow one. The neckline is the pivot between the two peaks, and nothing else. A shape is rejected when the peaks sit closer together than the minimum spacing, when the two legs differ in bar count by more than the leg-balance allowance, or when an optional prior trend into the pattern is absent. The trade triggers only on a bar closing through the neckline, with the option to wait for a pullback to that level instead, and it is abandoned if price violates the paired extreme or the confirmation window expires. Everything that follows is that contract expressed in code.
With the contract fixed, we begin the implementation with the foundation the rest of the program builds on: the trade library, the enumerations that expose our choices, the input set, and the global state that carries a pattern from detection through to entry.
