We start by including the standard "Trade.mqh" library for order execution, then declare three enumerations that shape the program's behavior. The "LotSizingMode" enumeration selects fixed or risk-based lots, the "TakeProfitMode"
enumeration
chooses between a measured-move target and a reward-to-risk multiple, and the "SetupState" enumeration names the three stages a setup passes through: idle, armed and waiting for the neckline break, and waiting for the pullback. That last enumeration is the backbone of the state machine that drives the whole entry sequence.
The inputs group tunable settings: pattern rules (tolerance, balance, spacing, prior-trend), plus stop, target, entry, trailing, and visual options. We then define the "SwingPoint" structure to hold a single confirmed pivot, its price, time, and whether it is a high or a low, since the whole detection works off a rolling history of these pivots. The global block that follows keeps that pivot buffer and the full state of the active pattern, from the two peaks and the neckline to the extreme we anchor the stop against and the counter that ages each stage, so every step of the setup has a fixed place to read and write as bars arrive.
Drawing the Pattern and Storing Swings
