We define the "DrawPattern"
function
to lay out the whole shape once a pattern is found, drawing the two legs, the horizontal neckline, an optional leading trend leg, and the peak and neckline markers with their labels, all flipped in orientation and color by whether it is a top or a bottom. The "ExtendNeckline" function keeps that neckline stretched to the current bar while we wait, so the trigger level stays visible as price approaches it, and the "DrawPullback" function traces the path from the breakout down to the retest so a pullback entry is easy to read after the fact. The one piece that matters beyond visuals is the "AddSwing" function: it appends each confirmed pivot to the "swings" history, ignores duplicates, and caps the buffer at eighty entries by dropping the oldest, which keeps the pattern search working off a bounded, recent set of pivots rather than growing without limit over a long run.
Detecting Swing Pivots
The pattern search runs entirely on swing pivots, so we define the "DetectSwings" function to confirm and store them one bar at a time.
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Detect a confirmed swing high or low and store it                |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
void
